A||N||3||burdell1||Bose Cube Surround Speakers||||5||
Z||000000||burdell1||02-19-2000||12:12 PM||||I'm trying to buy a cheap pair of surround speakers that look like the Bose style of double cube speakers. I bought a pair of Radio Shack ones once, but they were so faint that I could barely hear them (the ones i have now work pretty well, but they are kind of big for surround speakers.) has anyone bought these kind of speakers and tried putting new tweeters in them? I couldn't find any way of taking them apart? OR better yet, does anybody out there have the Bose kind for a reasonable (a.k.a. cheap) price? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Shiner||02-19-2000||04:34 PM||||Keep saving until you can afford those bose speakers!  They really are terrific surround speakers.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Robert||02-19-2000||05:15 PM||||I've got the single Bose cube speakers in my family room as part of a 5 speaker surround system.  Believe me, they sound great.  I did add a power sub, to add more punch to the videos I rent.   The sub that comes with the Bose system is not a power sub.  I put my old LARGE speakers out on the deck, where I have more room.  Go with the Bose system, I did a lot of checking before I bought mine, nothing sounded as good.<p>[This message has been edited by Robert (edited 02-19-2000).]||||reg||3
Z||000003||william_thorniii||02-21-2000||02:36 PM||||Gentlemen,<P>I used to sell both home and car audio and still consider myself somewhat of an audiophile.  I would never under any circumstances purchase anything manufactured by Bose.  That aside many people claim that they like them.  I will admit that Bose do have very clear high's, however; they are completely inadequate for rear speakers because it is physically impossible to reproduce the lower sounds with their 1.5" speakers.  So the effect is that only the high sounds can be reproduced with these speakers.  All of the other manufacturers use a tiny tweeter in addition to a speaker near 3-4".  Now while this does not seem like a large difference let me assure you that it is.  <P>What I reccomend you do is save up a couple of hundred $.  In audio equiptment there is no such thing as a "cheap solution".  The brand that I strongly reccomend for small speakers is Polk.  I think that for all around sound they beat just about any that I have heard including some exclusive brands such as: Acoustic Research, Boston Acoustics, JBL, Infinity & Klipsh.  For over all tonal quality is where the Polks accel.  <P>The problem is that with audio equiptment, each individual hears things differently.  Therefore you and only you can decide what brands produce sounds pleasing to you.  The only way to go about this is to get to one store that carries a few of the brand in a small room somewhat like where you keep your system.  If you have a Circuit City near you, go there first.  I have found that their rooms are best suited to getting a good representation of the sound that you might get at your own house.  Also at CC you can listen to JBL (least expensive), Infinity, Bose and Polk.  If you get a good salesman they can quickly switch between each of the pairs during the same song and keep all other variables the same so you can truly tell which sound is the best for you.<P>For the record, I am not an employee of CC, but they do have the best set-up IMHO for testing speakers that I have found in the Cincinnati-Dayton Area.<P>William T. Thorn, III ||||reg||12
